      "name": "WriteBitsWithByteStuffing",
      "signature": "int WriteBitsWithByteStuffing(uint param_1, int param_2, int param_3)",
      "calling_convention": "__cdecl",
      "return_type": "int",
      "comment": "Writes bits to JPEG output stream with JPEG byte stuffing (0xFF -> 0xFF 0x00).\n\nThis is an optimized MMX/SIMD version of WriteBitsToJpegStream that handles\nbyte stuffing inline for performance. Uses MM7 as a 64-bit bit accumulator.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Add nBitCount to accumulated bit count in buffer\n2. Shift dwBitData into MM7 bit accumulator at appropriate position\n3. If accumulated bits >= 32, write DWORD(s) to output buffer\n4. For each DWORD written, check all 4 bytes for 0xFF values\n5. If 0xFF found, insert 0x00 stuffing byte per JPEG spec\n6. Continue until accumulated bits < 32\n7. Update buffer write pointer and bit count\n\nParameters:\n  dwBitData    - Huffman-coded data bits to write (right-aligned)\n  nBitCount    - Number of valid bits in dwBitData (1-32)\n  pOutBuffer   - JPEG output buffer structure containing:\n                 +0x0C: nAccumBitCount - accumulated bits in MM7\n                 +0x10: pWritePos - current write position\n                 +0x18: pBufferEnd - buffer end boundary\n                 +0x1C: nBytesWritten - running count\n                 +0x40: abDataBuffer[0x1000] - actual data buffer\n\nReturns:\n  0 on success\n  Non-zero error code if buffer flush (FUN_60002140) fails\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - Uses PCMPEQB for parallel 0xFF detection across 4 bytes\n  - Different code paths for 1, 2, 3, or 4 bytes needing stuffing\n  - MM7 preserved across calls for bit accumulation (IMPLICIT in_MM7)\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n  0x40 (64) - Total bits in MM7 accumulator\n  0x20 (32) - DWORD size in bits, flush threshold\n  0x17 (23) - Minimum buffer space before flush required\n  0x1000 (4096) - Data buffer size\n  0xFF - JPEG marker prefix, triggers byte stuffing\n  0x00 - Stuffing byte inserted after 0xFF\n\nDecompiler Note:\n  This function uses heavy MMX operations. The decompiler generates\n  transient SSA variables (uVar1, cVar3, etc.) from MMX register\n  decomposition. These are compiler temporaries that cannot be\n  permanently renamed.",
