      "name": "WriteQuantizationTableMarker",
      "signature": "undefined WriteQuantizationTableMarker(undefined4 param_1, char param_2, char param_3, char * param_4)",
      "calling_convention": "__cdecl",
      "return_type": "undefined",
      "comment": "Writes a JPEG DQT (Define Quantization Table) marker segment to the output stream.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize local buffer pointer to stack-allocated marker data buffer\n2. Write DQT marker byte (0xDB) to buffer via WriteMarkerByte\n3. Write segment length (0x43 = 67 bytes) via WriteMarkerWord\n4. Combine precision (upper 4 bits) and table ID (lower 4 bits) into single byte\n5. Copy 64 quantization values from source table with stride 2 (zigzag reordering)\n6. Call encoder output function to write completed segment to output\n\nParameters:\npJpegContext - JpegImageContext pointer passed to output callback function\ncPrecision - Quantization value precision (0=8-bit, 1=16-bit), stored in upper nibble\ncTableId - Quantization table destination ID (0-3), stored in lower nibble\npbQuantizationTable - Source quantization table with values at stride 2 (interleaved zigzag order)\n\nReturns:\nvoid - No return value, marker data written through callback\n\nSpecial Cases:\nSource table uses stride 2 to skip zigzag index bytes in interleaved format\nBuffer size 72 bytes accommodates marker plus length plus precision/id plus 64 values\n\nMagic Numbers:\n0xDB - JPEG DQT (Define Quantization Table) marker\n0x43 - Segment length 67 bytes (2 length + 1 precision/id + 64 values)\n0x10 - Shift value for 4-bit precision field (cPrecision * 16)\n0x40 - Loop count for 64 quantization table entries\n0x45 - Output callback command flag for write operation",
      "name_source": "Classic/1.00",
      "method": "NOP",
